#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5498add-bc06-5903-9efe-ef81027091af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5498add-bc06-5903-9efe-ef81027091af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256443} :answer-field/id #uuid "540fe2c8-dadb-5db3-8dab-b24c95763f08" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256442}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3c8ab44-204b-5d29-bcb7-af809bc21a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-100273" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fda731fa3c9a3401e9b9e2a7d4c103829d3fe3b14ac9d6f2872b76a319f6ae91.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{3}-4(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "852449ca-b4df-5142-9540-bb503d5b7b62" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Negative Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed996f2-f9db-5f3a-9c53-ce638160902a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed996f2-f9db-5f3a-9c53-ce638160902a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256577} :answer-field/id #uuid "cad7fde3-82c0-59d2-800f-c2f0934ff17b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256576}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34bf4bc2-4f77-5ec2-b028-851ecd7a1784" :question/math-academy-id "q-102185" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{1}{4x},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4x}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-1){x}^{-1-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffc1d505-17e4-5b0f-9150-506f9edb3350" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1aaf8a-3d5f-5835-811e-3a659778203e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e1aaf8a-3d5f-5835-811e-3a659778203e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256592} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2c01cef-97c9-5591-9025-3a5af6e30b08" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256591}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2da3e499-592f-56ab-a8b2-fa769f0b592f" :question/math-academy-id "q-10289" :question/problem "Find the derivative of  $f(x)=4{x}^{6}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We apply the constant multiple and power rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6{x}^{6-1} \\\\  & =24{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b6d182-004d-566c-bf31-7147d1ecb86c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75{x}^{14}-32{x}^{7}+35{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b6d182-004d-566c-bf31-7147d1ecb86c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75{x}^{14}-32{x}^{7}+35{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256691} :answer-field/id #uuid "a91ce7e6-caa0-5d60-a558-541eae1f4ecf" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f25ae3e-b1ef-5b98-82be-6340c3c3c87e" :question/math-academy-id "q-10726" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=5{x}^{15}-4{x}^{8}+7{x}^{5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{15}-4{x}^{8}+7{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{15})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{8})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{15})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})+7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 15{x}^{(15-1)}-4\\cdot 8{x}^{(8-1)}+7\\cdot 5{x}^{(5-1)} \\\\  & =75{x}^{14}-32{x}^{7}+35{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016b1f26-e0db-5257-867e-b7eac24ccb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35{x}^{4}-6x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016b1f26-e0db-5257-867e-b7eac24ccb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35{x}^{4}-6x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256694} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdd19de4-1a36-51ea-8dd9-1bd4b8fc094c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256693}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36a4921a-bbad-5ced-a8e4-cc7bcc79bde0" :question/math-academy-id "q-10730" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}-5x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{5})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 5{x}^{(5-1)}-3\\cdot 2{x}^{(2-1)}-5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =35{x}^{4}-6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc0ce2c2-36b9-526b-93d6-31bf223ef326" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using the GCF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "537ab16a-e40c-5f97-a951-d58de51d5252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "537ab16a-e40c-5f97-a951-d58de51d5252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256887} :answer-field/id #uuid "070c4ccc-7e7e-523e-a68f-8973b72aec99" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256886}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65d26569-d024-5df5-80e7-ebdb725c458d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112805" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $2?$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $5{x}^{3}$ and $-15{x}^{2}$ is $5{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x-3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=3$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1100322-f9f9-5124-a50e-27f9ee343c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1100322-f9f9-5124-a50e-27f9ee343c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256899} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d9f829-2ab1-507e-a54e-e1c936f87e15" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256898}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e4c35d0-80ea-555f-a42d-fa766d0cba07" :question/math-academy-id "q-112867" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, What could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+10?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{-5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51b3db7c-057e-5c4b-934f-a7beba01180a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51b3db7c-057e-5c4b-934f-a7beba01180a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902} :answer-field/id #uuid "2935c1f0-080f-504b-bd7e-60952c1de321" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256901}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "284a91b3-0aac-5ff1-be86-b1f45987e7b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112871" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, What could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-12{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}-3x+10?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d247b06a-64f3-560a-a0df-357046193290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d247b06a-64f3-560a-a0df-357046193290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256905} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f63a45-44a6-5c2e-990a-9e8fb70acb02" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256904}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25b2159c-942d-5822-b9a6-b71a06f24251" :question/math-academy-id "q-112878" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, What **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{5}-5{x}^{3}+6?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $4$ does not take the above form. The value $4$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{4}{1},$ and the numerator $(4)$ is not a factor of $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8058df30-04de-555c-adfa-61fbf2b0ff31" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in One Variable With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "501932a3-ba5d-56e0-a8d4-32cd9e20e496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "501932a3-ba5d-56e0-a8d4-32cd9e20e496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256925} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bee3d3b-1394-5c6f-9067-2e4105836080" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256924}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "783bfb90-6184-5e8a-b664-e0e6d1dc5933" :question/math-academy-id "q-113228" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{3}{2z}=p$ and $\\frac{2}{z}=q,$ what is $p-q?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\np-q=\\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{z}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $2z$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{4}{2z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3-4}{2z} & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2z} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb2231aa-8afa-57df-a43c-cb3add25b2cc" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9c560f5-a103-57a7-a6db-bee28965fb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c560f5-a103-57a7-a6db-bee28965fb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256937} :answer-field/id #uuid "1daf8d4e-f8da-5961-af79-01959a2b049b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256936}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae19e4b0-11a7-5a6d-8c85-2a0f9d7eb465" :question/math-academy-id "q-113311" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{m-4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $n(m-4)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{m-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{n}{m-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n\\cdot n}{n(m-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "add45606-39f6-5a61-9535-867d2950d183" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc4ecf5b-73dd-5f51-a27b-6fe96c61b5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ecf5b-73dd-5f51-a27b-6fe96c61b5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256976} :answer-field/id #uuid "190a0b49-3535-5497-9cf4-9b9e7e6b42c5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256975}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e26de700-a75c-58a7-9db4-45270d8fe36b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113737" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {x}^{-11}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-11+1}}{-11+1})+C \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-10}}{-10})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{10}\\cdot {x}^{-10}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58e2e40e-209b-5bc7-98df-11841c2bbcc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f01617ca-9501-5b8a-82c2-89d9dc47e19e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f01617ca-9501-5b8a-82c2-89d9dc47e19e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257036} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b4d01d-0a3e-55dd-8bb1-80e8e50310d9" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257035} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158bf710-9ae8-5ca9-a8a3-0069a5332089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5d}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "158bf710-9ae8-5ca9-a8a3-0069a5332089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5d}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257038} :answer-field/id #uuid "76722a3b-6111-5612-be4f-fa0048eb8261" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1b6dcd8-b86b-57fe-bcef-873b76734928" :question/math-academy-id "q-113962" :question/problem "Find the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(2c,-d)$ and $(6c,6d).$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(2c,-d)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(6c,6d)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2c+6c}{2},\\frac{-d+6d}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8c}{2},\\frac{5d}{2}) \\\\  & =(4c,\\frac{5d}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db0f4fee-52ce-59ea-a878-1cc3251e9c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db0f4fee-52ce-59ea-a878-1cc3251e9c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257293} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ebf1136-0ad1-5236-b788-da4efd706743" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257292}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ae8950d-1f55-5b0d-b4d8-6562835c999a" :question/math-academy-id "q-118839" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $A(8,-3,-1)$ and $B(12,5,-2)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(8,-3,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(12,5,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(12-8)^{2}+(5-(-3))^{2}+(-2-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e178c7-bdb4-5139-a20c-3e17de51ee11" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df561d3-5704-567e-9bea-30eaabf51a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0df561d3-5704-567e-9bea-30eaabf51a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257309} :answer-field/id #uuid "47bec473-b654-5bf5-8f7f-7ce0b93b538f" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee79302d-cad7-5427-88cd-f0908fb97959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee79302d-cad7-5427-88cd-f0908fb97959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257311} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8f41b8-b188-56fc-ab1f-eefeefda3503" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257310}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a478ded4-a3e7-57e8-9ba2-a2eb8cb92b12" :question/math-academy-id "q-118955" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(12w,2w,-4w)$ and $B(7w,-6w,2w).$ Given that the distance between $A$ and $B$ is $30\\sqrt{5},$ find the possible values of $w.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(12w,2w,-4w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(7w,-6w,2w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(7w-12w)^{2}+(-6w-2w)^{2}+(2w-(-4w))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5w)^{2}+(-8w)^{2}+(6w)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{w}^{2}+64{w}^{2}+36{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125}\\,\\sqrt{{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=30\\sqrt{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w| & =30\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |w| & =6 \\\\ w & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f88bd4db-8477-5822-9cda-a608e78039e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93539bab-ab03-5a6a-8270-eae92298c289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{66}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93539bab-ab03-5a6a-8270-eae92298c289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{66}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257332} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed79f543-a4c1-5874-b52f-ef24cdd5a6f3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257331}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f19f56b-b91e-5f5c-acd5-5bfff04f8d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-119346" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a058d9b800decdb32fdd09715f1bebab03b4a12c7e6119d6c45bbb170ce640b0.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ in the diagram above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is equal to the distance between $P$ and $Q.$\n\nThe distance $d$ between two points $P({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $Q({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ have coordinates ${{(6,\\,0,\\,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and ${{(-1,4,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =\\sqrt{(-1-6)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}+(2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-7)^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{66}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57bae420-d529-5b9d-a306-37127c523be6" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule of Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4240970-1c4e-5367-8994-1981ec63f032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,456" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4240970-1c4e-5367-8994-1981ec63f032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,456" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257478} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fac9a33-1beb-5d7f-94e8-9a82cdb6858d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257477}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfa71c25-63e9-530b-b8d9-c3e3f8a5bfb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122144" :question/problem "How many possible outcomes are there if a coin is flipped twice, a six-sided die is rolled three times, and a tetrahedral (four-sided) die is rolled once?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "For the coin, there are $2$ outcomes: \"Heads\" and \"Tails\".\n\nFor the six-sided die, there is a total of $6$ outcomes: $1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\nFor the tetrahedral die, there is a total of $4$ outcomes: $1,2,3,4.$\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total Possible Outcomes} & =\\text{Number of outcomes when the coin is flipped the first time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the coin is flipped the second time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the first time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the second time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the third time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the tetrahedral die is rolled the one time} \\\\  & =2\\times 2\\times 6\\times 6\\times 6\\times 4 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\times {6}^{3}\\times 4 \\\\  & =4\\times 216\\times 4 \\\\  & =3\\,456.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $3\\,456$ possible outcomes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6699397d-0014-5749-b439-4a8486990986" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db6b9649-01d4-59e3-b7fa-9f83f933b5fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db6b9649-01d4-59e3-b7fa-9f83f933b5fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257502} :answer-field/id #uuid "351223ef-4f05-520b-aa60-8facfda88416" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257501}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92ff5704-f3f3-59c9-aa47-1a38090b6514" :question/math-academy-id "q-123598" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${210}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94582577-1f04-5709-b464-129e6784c987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94582577-1f04-5709-b464-129e6784c987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257508} :answer-field/id #uuid "e393ba74-6a5a-5da9-ae7e-262394091e01" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257507}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b889e158-30a8-50db-9eb5-59129862f7fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-123602" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d1066e2-91d8-5669-ac31-819b92dcca1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d1066e2-91d8-5669-ac31-819b92dcca1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257517} :answer-field/id #uuid "55c28b4d-6cd9-5534-b503-9d27638f083c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81b7ba65-677a-5040-93a4-08e58f005da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123607" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecd367ac-2a68-56d0-b553-85277a9c921d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Secant or Cosecant of a Special Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9279b7ff-0245-5cb3-9849-3b302d7cfed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9279b7ff-0245-5cb3-9849-3b302d7cfed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257526} :answer-field/id #uuid "00848923-134a-591b-9f79-eeaf5542302f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257525}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96dfea7f-c886-5a5c-b9c1-e0c3feccb5bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-123610" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{csc}{210}^{∘}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${210}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}{210}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecd367ac-2a68-56d0-b553-85277a9c921d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Secant or Cosecant of a Special Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5267510e-a3a4-5e30-8424-31ec204c44a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5267510e-a3a4-5e30-8424-31ec204c44a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257529} :answer-field/id #uuid "76217c3e-861b-5847-ac76-e520e20e7213" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257528}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba4b1499-2c1f-5119-a1e9-d5d6d0a91c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-123611" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{csc}{300}^{∘}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${300}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}{300}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}{300}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}{300}^{∘}}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecd367ac-2a68-56d0-b553-85277a9c921d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Secant or Cosecant of a Special Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f527be-209f-512b-970a-994469277415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5f527be-209f-512b-970a-994469277415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257538} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a988b62-67d1-560e-a847-10ee6a20c795" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257537}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3e46c86-6a9a-574d-be10-e9c92592678d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123615" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a119150-6d23-59bd-90c9-699de3a75795" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277fe498-4581-5f21-95c4-6f79b9079653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257643} {:answer/id #uuid "e4540e51-ac0f-5103-82ae-52e2c4b4ca7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257644} {:answer/id #uuid "7c337c75-434e-521e-9d75-6f50d7c65f1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257645} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5b4ba8-1253-5de3-9290-964e309dd27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e5b4ba8-1253-5de3-9290-964e309dd27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257646} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8b74f5-0ae4-561b-8237-eb3f0410635a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257642}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0de550b-4b2d-5fea-ac83-9bb3336e8c0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124417" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta ={660}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${660}^{∘}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- ${660}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}={300}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${300}^{∘}$ is coterminal with ${660}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bf700a84746b0764a3d2d1146f7eea6fe4722aa363af30ae065c228f04b31b7d.png)\n\nWe see that ${300}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, ${660}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a119150-6d23-59bd-90c9-699de3a75795" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4237479-2d96-5c1c-9f7d-03c10bb0c14f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257655} {:answer/id #uuid "20210c22-913d-50fa-877b-6bc30e54c9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257656} {:answer/id #uuid "d635c60c-4f5c-5c4a-a2c6-82edfa62c909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257657} {:answer/id #uuid "39a4ce2c-8592-5e52-a6b1-3cdd5f857792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20210c22-913d-50fa-877b-6bc30e54c9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257656} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8b71ec-17f5-5450-a870-ebf59e71234a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257654}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f569fd32-3924-5a95-b0a8-af4940374871" :question/math-academy-id "q-124420" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta ={760}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${760}^{∘}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- ${760}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}={400}^{∘}$\n\n- ${760}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={40}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${40}^{∘}$ is coterminal with ${760}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/5a371bf4b096c910668d7d1269b5d484f0a0cad5570d0a9ef9fc3806b7b03e18.png)\n\nWe see that ${40}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant I. Therefore, ${760}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99fb90fd-4eef-58fd-8fca-8f1d47988f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257713} {:answer/id #uuid "6f26502d-c71d-5b2e-af32-b17010a3f756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257714} {:answer/id #uuid "69115848-ea6d-5953-bb32-bccba4c3250e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257715} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6483ef-d505-5969-b996-0b0edd3f1d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f26502d-c71d-5b2e-af32-b17010a3f756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257714} :answer-field/id #uuid "713cd814-f1b5-5784-a54e-b1bff8791608" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257712}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aef049cc-770d-5d6c-beb4-622b042039bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-124710" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-{675}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{305}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{675}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{315}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{675}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={45}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${45}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{675}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e9e985d1b9787a05c97ead1fa6d80ef411bc1a636bd7a670a221d663d7041f5c.png)\n\nWe see that ${45}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant I. Therefore, $-{675}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba0ad98-6940-502a-873c-8a589a03a499" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49336535-e555-5207-80c7-d481684e5204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{107}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49336535-e555-5207-80c7-d481684e5204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{107}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257725} :answer-field/id #uuid "833f060b-aa16-538c-b30b-99a64a409ff7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257724}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "760dac55-8601-5be5-8581-6b78da458ce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124735" :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with ${467}^{∘}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in degrees), we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to ${467}^{∘}$:\n\n- ${467}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={827}^{∘}$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than ${827}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from ${467}^{∘}$:\n\n- ${467}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}={107}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\n- ${467}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}=-{253}^{∘}$\n\n- ${467}^{∘}-3\\cdot {360}^{∘}=-{613}^{∘}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-{613}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is ${107}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c35f8dc-22de-5a14-9ebc-39437f22ddbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c35f8dc-22de-5a14-9ebc-39437f22ddbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257734} :answer-field/id #uuid "62938770-04ab-5d54-a268-d5b4e1f01fae" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257733}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "458a2a1e-c894-53df-8dbd-7679592e8bbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-124758" :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{3}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{11\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{11\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{17\\pi }{3}$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\frac{17\\pi }{3},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{11\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{11\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$\n\n- $\\frac{11\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $\\frac{11\\pi }{3}-3\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{7\\pi }{3},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "936442df-acdc-5d67-8bd9-1964d9dfeefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "936442df-acdc-5d67-8bd9-1964d9dfeefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257737} :answer-field/id #uuid "af894760-2eea-57c1-a63c-2c80af55d8b8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257736}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35635b29-ef8a-58d1-829e-341b9dc52e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-124760" :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\frac{13\\pi }{3},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\n\n- $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot 2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $\\frac{7\\pi }{3}-3\\cdot 2\\pi =-\\frac{11\\pi }{3}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{11\\pi }{3},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92c49ea0-7ec5-5ffc-8a36-c4f46f78fda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e75d8e1d-4242-5c15-8568-7a957c4a9719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e75d8e1d-4242-5c15-8568-7a957c4a9719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258035} :answer-field/id #uuid "24760f79-1c23-51bb-b2df-97129dc228c1" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffb907ea-d641-581c-98cf-1b962e5e9dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffb907ea-d641-581c-98cf-1b962e5e9dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258037} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cc5099b-40eb-57c9-a42e-d9bb31902824" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258036}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30fb72b2-8ceb-5d3e-98ff-4e76c71e39e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-13602" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ba20fb3746240004550da009e8cefa3437d559bb9f33e9cf42e97800fbddabec.png)\n\nFind the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints shown above.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between the given points ${{(-2,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(4,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-2+4}{2},\\frac{3-2}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(1,\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9bfd9e9-603e-50ae-8cd1-be79b88aa087" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Leading Coefficients as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea150614-6020-58e8-8084-1b4e7ab56aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(9+{x}^{2})(3+x)(3-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea150614-6020-58e8-8084-1b4e7ab56aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(9+{x}^{2})(3+x)(3-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258101} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc52295b-34ed-5380-8dfe-34b13cdffe21" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258100}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0260538c-f491-56b1-ab37-bac54b1dad98" :question/math-academy-id "q-14137" :question/problem "Factor $243-3{x}^{4}$ completely.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $3.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n243-3{x}^{4}=3\\cdot 81-3\\cdot {x}^{4}=3(81-{x}^{4})\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n3(81-{x}^{4})=3({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n3({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})=3(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2}) & =3(9+{x}^{2})({3}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3(9+{x}^{2})(3+x)(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97261046-84b7-54d0-ae71-920ee699e7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97261046-84b7-54d0-ae71-920ee699e7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258107} :answer-field/id #uuid "91cda7c9-e9f2-5ab9-99fe-1f74848e6195" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258106}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2543d42-472f-5dce-a205-70e0b82fc936" :question/math-academy-id "q-14140" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm \\frac{6}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm \\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1\\,\\pm \\frac{6}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{12}{5},\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $24$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 12=24.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92c49ea0-7ec5-5ffc-8a36-c4f46f78fda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6747ecdd-9afe-5723-ad80-bae5a0753706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6747ecdd-9afe-5723-ad80-bae5a0753706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258310} :answer-field/id #uuid "57926a54-2a90-573e-9bac-f527f0037ebc" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e9e03c-28e2-5529-876b-ca1c1e0944d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e9e03c-28e2-5529-876b-ca1c1e0944d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258312} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15efcc2-8aec-5cd7-8978-77caf324cd6d" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258311}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06a9a618-4104-5c38-8036-d75acee7cee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-14358" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/781f3a15f9c0b8ac1d43e72f0911bc810dc9829f37bf64094a9d1d48f8a7e1f1.png)\n\nFind the midpoint of the line segment shown above.\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between the given points ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-3,6)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{7-3}{2},\\frac{-4+6}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2},\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  & =(2,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6f4cfa-db03-5ae6-9f43-79fd3167c473" :knowledge-point/title "The Vertical Asymptote of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f493eca-3a21-51c3-92f4-40a48a8146a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f493eca-3a21-51c3-92f4-40a48a8146a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258610} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf3ba6e-8536-5f0d-84b8-ba0c8d98e218" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "540e0beb-27dc-5689-9b70-9acb574ebe65" :question/math-academy-id "q-166547" :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=-{log}_{2}(9-3x)+4.$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y={log}_{2}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-3x & =0 \\\\ -3x & =-9 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2229bd-c425-5e53-b8cf-56f3f9f3f539" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Polynomial Using Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00db8438-15de-5d14-b327-9416a8d364f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400a+20b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00db8438-15de-5d14-b327-9416a8d364f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400a+20b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258645} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b356e3b-1ab5-5b61-8661-26162f98a2e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258644}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75ec1bc2-8651-5192-b499-b0ffe6eb2c97" :question/math-academy-id "q-167208" :question/problem "In terms of $a$ and $b,\\,p(20)=\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=20$ into the expression for $p(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =a{x}^{2}+bx \\\\ p(20) & =a(20)^{2}+b(20) \\\\  & =400a+20b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(20)=400a+20b.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "075c23a4-9de9-59b5-bec4-575b0b0454f4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82cd1908-0024-5479-8067-6288c85edcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240-20a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82cd1908-0024-5479-8067-6288c85edcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240-20a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258648} :answer-field/id #uuid "b707568f-bbe6-532e-bb58-23348cdbf806" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258647}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3fc19ec2-9fcf-558b-bcfa-fe08c1b1d5c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-167209" :question/problem "If the bakery sells twenty cakes in a week, the weekly profit will be $$4\\,800.$ Find an expression for $b$ in terms of $a.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $p(20)=400a+20b.$ So, we need to solve the equation $400a+20b=4\\,800$ for the parameter $b.$\n\nWe need to isolate the variable $b.$ To do this, we treat $a$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $400a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}400a+20b & =4\\,800 \\\\ 400a+20b-400a & =4\\,800-400a \\\\ 20b & =4\\,800-400a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b & =4\\,800-400a \\\\ \\frac{20b}{20} & =\\frac{4\\,800-400a}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{20}b}{\\cancel{20}} & =\\frac{4\\,800}{20}-\\frac{400a}{20} \\\\ b & =240-20a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $b=240-20a.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "708a0248-6941-505b-81cb-fac6b0e13ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "708a0248-6941-505b-81cb-fac6b0e13ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a946c9-fc0f-55fa-b14d-b6d33d7911be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a194f5e9-9385-5cd1-ad1a-5979b68b20d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a194f5e9-9385-5cd1-ad1a-5979b68b20d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258653} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a8ef031-bf8e-58d6-9c2c-ab33d25b38e5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258652}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3745304a-337c-57fe-9274-0c5590dd8f9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-167210" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $30b+900a=6\\,000.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=240-20a \\\\ 30b+900a=6\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=240-20a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30b+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 30(240-20a)+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200-600a+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200+300a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 300a & =-1\\,200 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-4$ into $b=240-20a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =240-20(-4) \\\\ b & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=320.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3e0464-63c9-5eef-bc9e-5c1c2afcd9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a3e0464-63c9-5eef-bc9e-5c1c2afcd9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258656} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4fb357e-085a-5f3b-b537-38769b8780f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258655}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b9d6db3-e650-565d-8f8c-e2de39a23110" :question/math-academy-id "q-167211" :question/problem "Determine the weekly profit if the bakery produces $35$ cakes in a week.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-4{x}^{2}+320x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=35$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(35) & =-4(35)^{2}+320(35) \\\\  & =-4\\,900+11\\,200 \\\\  & =6\\,300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$6\\,300.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1134c0-02ce-59c1-8238-4820da388f90" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Monomial From a Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7b1e001-fc51-5fb9-8408-2afdb4d08019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-80x+1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7b1e001-fc51-5fb9-8408-2afdb4d08019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-80x+1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258659} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e8a5cb2-86eb-5315-b18c-bbfd63944604" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258658}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6d32981-6a85-569d-bdb7-7f0b327bbe43" :question/math-academy-id "q-167212" :question/problem "The manager wants to determine the number of cakes the bakery should produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$6\\,000.$ The required number of cakes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$4\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We factor $4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(4{x}^{2},320x,6\\,000)=4\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000 & = \\\\ 4\\cdot ({x}^{2})+4\\cdot (-80x)+4\\cdot (1\\,500)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({x}^{2})+4\\cdot (-80x)+4\\cdot (1\\,500) & = \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c9ba00d-9171-5732-8539-2e2943cce58e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Trinomials With Positive Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1be701-b65f-52a8-b5ad-99d023b13697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a1be701-b65f-52a8-b5ad-99d023b13697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258662} :answer-field/id #uuid "8edca494-ef17-5230-bf6f-214bfc5a0fd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258661}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7095629a-fbfb-5e1c-bf46-32aaaf63e2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-167213" :question/problem "Find the minimum number of cakes the bakery needs to produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$6\\,000.$\n\nThe bakery needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ cakes per week." :question/worked-solution "From part 5, the required number of cakes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4\\cdot ({x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(1\\,500)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-80).$ The required factors are $(-30)$ and $(-50).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-80x=-30x-50x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-30x-50x+1\\,500 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-30x-50x+1\\,500 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-30x)+(-50x+1\\,500) & =0 \\\\ x(x-30)-50(x-30) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-50)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=30$ or $x=50.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the bakery needs to produce $30$ cakes per week to make a profit of $$6\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36e1e439-ec4a-5577-80d5-3b113bf36e74" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Recursive Formula to an Explicit Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a711f70d-38d1-50cb-a94d-e15aadec19bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4n+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a711f70d-38d1-50cb-a94d-e15aadec19bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4n+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258665} :answer-field/id #uuid "14145251-4438-553d-b8a7-49e37064d2fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258664}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "977c6875-4975-5233-bc94-e060e1ce6574" :question/math-academy-id "q-167214" :question/problem "The bakery wants to increase its output by implementing a new production model. The number of cakes ${a}_{n}$ produced in the $n$th week since the implementation of the new model is given by the arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+4,\\,{a}_{1}=19,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nWhat is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence?\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=19,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+4 \\\\  & =19+4 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =23-19 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute ${a}_{1}=19$ and $d=4$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =19+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =19+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=4n+15,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a560db4d-e9d3-5d89-b2cd-c886f32db9df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fdc6b0a-3041-5128-91c6-15cccec4bf0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fdc6b0a-3041-5128-91c6-15cccec4bf0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258703} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2533ba-03c9-5b80-bda3-73df89b22c4f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258702}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70bfc310-494e-53ee-86b4-a9541d3696d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-170491" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=-2-2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d1c9cb7e7b00a11e00eafb4b51696c74c2a0cfb6a41c69cf27ecd011c382bfec.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54588722-bb80-54ca-b100-03aca39e0c17" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d07a788-7c1d-5a77-a267-41ffbdc311fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d07a788-7c1d-5a77-a267-41ffbdc311fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258706} :answer-field/id #uuid "0622ec40-59a3-58fc-8295-363ef0b69c8f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258705}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82e6558d-3a1c-5d3b-8169-608f8ebc1f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-170493" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=3-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d58ec3abe2b06d39ec4b8f66e785d433f6bfa6b8eb52faab98aae306a63debe5.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57bae420-d529-5b9d-a306-37127c523be6" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule of Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d69ac29-79f0-5fcb-975f-beec98dd346e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d69ac29-79f0-5fcb-975f-beec98dd346e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755} :answer-field/id #uuid "d535795e-b9e8-5397-b1c7-31bd591797df" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258754}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "013caba4-8d5e-572a-9ea0-5bedf0d0d6bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1809" :question/problem "Rio's Pizza offers a mix and match pizza on its menu. There are $4$ different meat types to choose from, $5$ different vegetables, $4$ different types of cheese, and $2$ different types of crust. How many different types of pizza can be made by choosing a type of meat, a vegetable, cheese and a crust?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of pizzas} & =\\text{Number of type of meats} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of type of vegetables} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of type of cheese} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of type of crust} \\\\  & =4\\times 5\\times 4\\times 2 \\\\  & =160.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160$ types of pizza can be made with the given ingredients."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58e2e40e-209b-5bc7-98df-11841c2bbcc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e57325-b08d-5553-aceb-ddf9a3846d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90e57325-b08d-5553-aceb-ddf9a3846d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258909} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8bbaf97-9f4b-5151-9c89-d3af09f4f8ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258908} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ef209db-5bb3-563d-a1c8-300440619571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ef209db-5bb3-563d-a1c8-300440619571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258911} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e22324d-6b0b-5b6f-aa7f-fafb33b0ad72" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258910}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01cffbc0-2831-535f-b29d-c07d42307600" :question/math-academy-id "q-216930" :question/problem "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(-3a,6b)$ and $(3a,8b)$ is$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(-3a,6b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(3a,8b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-3a+3a}{2},\\frac{6b+8b}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{0}{2},\\frac{14b}{2}) \\\\  & =(0,7b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b6b70eb-df57-5d17-a753-645ccdf94945" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of an Endpoint Given the Midpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c279cc70-3deb-5b9e-91de-00cbf4471ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c279cc70-3deb-5b9e-91de-00cbf4471ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258914} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8797e1-0608-5803-a821-b6d7a0bd78fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258913} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a6ab656-6512-5b5b-ac92-20827de4f1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a6ab656-6512-5b5b-ac92-20827de4f1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258916} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae4ec966-50a9-58f4-9052-6a7926f19429" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258915}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0073fa29-c764-515b-a4c4-0453628fa131" :question/math-academy-id "q-216947" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/226b832db682da8379d9567123974abb3d44c02f4db3c3ef78d48c1a8f2a491e.png)\n\nGiven that $(1,1)$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ above, find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The given points are ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, ${{(-2,0)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(1,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (1,1) & =(\\frac{a-2}{2},\\frac{b+0}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=\\frac{a-2}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,{\\begin{aligned}a=4 \\\\ b=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a=4$ and $b=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b6b70eb-df57-5d17-a753-645ccdf94945" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of an Endpoint Given the Midpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "910578dd-3cda-5f54-8d99-4d4c3eda1586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "910578dd-3cda-5f54-8d99-4d4c3eda1586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258919} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c41bb54-4d3c-5605-aaf4-e47f420f4817" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258918} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a14855c-1819-5efb-841e-585aff62248b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a14855c-1819-5efb-841e-585aff62248b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258921} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4ea0ab-50b3-5afa-a20a-67b6c895420f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258920}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a304c4c4-c219-59bd-a8bd-8b7e75adfe38" :question/math-academy-id "q-216950" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1d287d5174be8420fd3251add91fb5da1e92a20e96ce4a4e9d9aac884cd13f49.png)\n\nGiven that $(1,1)$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ above, find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The given points are ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, ${{(-3,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(1,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (1,1) & =(\\frac{a-3}{2},\\frac{b+3}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=\\frac{a-3}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{b+3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,{\\begin{aligned}a=5 \\\\ b=-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a=5$ and $b=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31fad914-85aa-5b16-8a3e-0c5df29d84a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31fad914-85aa-5b16-8a3e-0c5df29d84a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258954} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed0baf4a-3a0b-508a-a293-ccd755201b9e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258953}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2723d41-7fa1-5f76-8187-90256555fb34" :question/math-academy-id "q-23381" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Sequence Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5e608a-153a-5c4e-9e29-86a334e6405b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f5e608a-153a-5c4e-9e29-86a334e6405b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258975} :answer-field/id #uuid "647f2265-2d0b-595f-8607-f41893c6720e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258974}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e45b9976-fa3e-51c4-b89b-76df6fe75175" :question/math-academy-id "q-237441" :question/problem "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=-2$ and ${a}_{2}=0,$ then ${a}_{4}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To compute ${a}_{4},$ we first need to compute ${a}_{3}.$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =0+(-2) \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute ${a}_{4},$ we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2+2} & ={a}_{2+1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =-2+0 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e397bdab-1908-5f2a-a002-238465d43b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e397bdab-1908-5f2a-a002-238465d43b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258978} :answer-field/id #uuid "251a271f-5d1f-5a5e-a916-0846c8540000" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258977}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76ea3a41-e453-5c70-bd88-235147c5e101" :question/math-academy-id "q-237453" :question/problem "If\n$f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)$\nwith\n$f(1)=0$ and $f(2)=5,$\nthen $f(6)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The recursion relation is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =5+0 \\\\ f(3) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & =5+5 \\\\ f(4) & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe continue this process to compute $f(5)$ and $f(6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =f(3+2)=f(3+1)+f(3)=f(4)+f(3)=10+5=15 \\\\ f(6) & =f(4+2)=f(4+1)+f(4)=f(5)+f(4)=15+10=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(6)=25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcea349e-5f0b-53d1-8daf-f94ceb654fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcea349e-5f0b-53d1-8daf-f94ceb654fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258981} :answer-field/id #uuid "847a808d-84db-5140-bb36-fd9378f94bb9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258980}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95f1e4d2-2432-5d53-b0c0-3cad912edf28" :question/math-academy-id "q-237456" :question/problem "The recursive formula for the Fibonacci numbers is \n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n),\\,f(1)=f(2)=1,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe $6$th term of the sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The recursion relation is\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =1+1 \\\\ f(3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, substituting $n=2,3,4$ into the recursive formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =f(2+2)=f(2+1)+f(2)=f(3)+f(2)=2+1=3 \\\\ f(5) & =f(3+2)=f(3+1)+f(3)=f(4)+f(3)=3+2=5 \\\\ f(6) & =f(4+2)=f(4+1)+f(4)=f(5)+f(4)=5+3=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $6$th term of the sequence is $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f88bd4db-8477-5822-9cda-a608e78039e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b355e889-4e22-5abc-be28-c9da0a87aaf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b355e889-4e22-5abc-be28-c9da0a87aaf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258990} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f3e07a-f9ca-5f56-896b-e1a426c76290" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258989}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfd37c70-8e31-5603-92e3-2ed3eefa553a" :question/math-academy-id "q-241749" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/163faea5580d664bc6ef0e34497f2768c8dd95489687269f0a145df185db5956.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is equal to the distance between $A$ and $B.$\n\nThe distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ and $B$ have coordinates ${{(1,\\,3,\\,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and ${{(-2,1,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{(-2-1)^{2}+(1-3)^{2}+(2-(-4))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bf2677e-830a-5df9-84ce-c07ab3aa9178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bf2677e-830a-5df9-84ce-c07ab3aa9178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258993} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a3e44d-f501-5630-9b08-b74bd4d99276" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258992}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2eef1bb5-5c70-50ac-9324-f327755b7e1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-241753" :question/problem "The distance between the points $A(4,1,-3)$ and $B(2,1,3)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(4,1,-3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(2,1,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-4)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}+(3-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e178c7-bdb4-5139-a20c-3e17de51ee11" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a1cea69-a61d-5531-b135-73ab05ec8a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a1cea69-a61d-5531-b135-73ab05ec8a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258996} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f2e6c10-05cc-56d0-856a-73d603334b64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d59deb38-bf0a-5654-a577-8863de98232f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d59deb38-bf0a-5654-a577-8863de98232f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258998} :answer-field/id #uuid "556725c1-e553-5b68-8fc5-a57c07be185b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258997}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14453831-d8b8-5b5b-aadc-087311354357" :question/math-academy-id "q-241755" :question/problem "If the distance between the points $A(-p,p,3p)$ and $B(3p,3p,-p)$ is $30,$ then the possible values of $p,$ **in ascending order**, are\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and\n$\\,p=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(-p,p,3p)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(3p,3p,-p)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(3p-(-p))^{2}+(3p-p)^{2}+(-p-3p)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4p)^{2}+(2p)^{2}+(-4p)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{p}^{2}+4{p}^{2}+16{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{p}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\,|p|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=30,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\,|p| & =30 \\\\ |p| & =5 \\\\ p & =\\pm 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the possible values of $p,$ in ascending order, are\n\n$$\np=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,p=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7b8626-be4e-5481-b8fd-2247386bd4f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af7b8626-be4e-5481-b8fd-2247386bd4f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259154} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c2c71cd-fb0e-58d5-a813-5a77ccdaef38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3884c922-f158-5490-8c69-dd3c8a9750e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-275157" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-4+6\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/325444dbc509efd27f6c5bf16c751b137532e2566a9dfaa69f92d9ce16bac51b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{6}{4}) \\\\  & =0.982\\text{ }793…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi -0.982\\text{ }793…\\approx 2.16,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54588722-bb80-54ca-b100-03aca39e0c17" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daa37f5e-6550-5c9b-90ca-3f018f7d292e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa37f5e-6550-5c9b-90ca-3f018f7d292e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259157} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9cb8ee3-ce50-5540-b6cf-da4c24340687" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259156}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d20bc44d-b47a-5708-be0b-150f38a3bde3" :question/math-academy-id "q-275159" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=9-7\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b6ec4332891a4c7684be8de2ca94bf24a1d9abd5170d9fe38ad3a1346afae56a.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{7}{9})=0.661\\,043…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=2\\pi -0.661\\,043\\approx 5.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf469ac-a510-5ce0-9a82-a32665a0ccc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bf469ac-a510-5ce0-9a82-a32665a0ccc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259187} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f987026-e95a-5fcd-a245-183915aeb393" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259186}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d66368db-ce50-599d-b4ed-96dcd1390201" :question/math-academy-id "q-276814" :question/problem "The multiplicity of the root $x=1$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-11x+6$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $x=1$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-11$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}+5x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}+5x-6).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-1)(x-1)(x+6) \\\\  & =(x+6)(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80880acc-6a2f-568b-a091-3b3f5ff8c0bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80880acc-6a2f-568b-a091-3b3f5ff8c0bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259193} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a0e3d2-a0da-504b-bd42-b8dc646ed762" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259192}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11643b7e-f172-5ec5-8c9a-009f4edf0ce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-276827" :question/problem "The multiplicity of the root $x=-5$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-15x-100$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $x=-5$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x+5)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+5)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+5)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-5$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-15$ | $-100$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $-5$ | $100$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-20$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x+5}={x}^{2}+x-20,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x+5)({x}^{2}+x-20).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x+5)(x-4)(x+5) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80670920-6ea4-5513-9ff8-0666a5ab5a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80670920-6ea4-5513-9ff8-0666a5ab5a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259290} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e7b2d0-75e8-57df-bb5c-be7d6ceb344f" :answer-field/key "negative-imaginary" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259289} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce5d334d-56cb-50ab-9973-123e41f4dbd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce5d334d-56cb-50ab-9973-123e41f4dbd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259292} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f7c406-dba3-5e9d-b52e-eb2f71f3a818" :answer-field/key "positive-imaginary" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259291}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d48e2ba-01ce-5260-8b5c-13f37e1e6682" :question/math-academy-id "q-28178" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\,-6x=-{x}^{2}-13.$\n\nNegative imaginary: {{negative-imaginary}}\n\nPositive imaginary: {{positive-imaginary}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+13=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=-6,$ and $c=13.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-6)\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4(1)(13)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{36-52}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=3+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=3-2\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "855e59cf-f2dc-54f0-a4c9-fda962fc6e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "855e59cf-f2dc-54f0-a4c9-fda962fc6e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259295} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb0fe08-1cd4-5f26-b858-4fed365eb78e" :answer-field/key "negative-imaginary" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6d028d3-e3cb-5ca9-b1fa-bd692712e892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6d028d3-e3cb-5ca9-b1fa-bd692712e892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259297} :answer-field/id #uuid "29b7bbcf-7bd1-562a-a3c0-9d7dcd78211e" :answer-field/key "positive-imaginary" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259296}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cce1890b-f4b6-5a39-8f00-affe971dd43b" :question/math-academy-id "q-28183" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\,{x}^{2}+2x=-50.$\n\nNegative imaginary: {{negative-imaginary}}\n\nPositive imaginary: {{positive-imaginary}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+50=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=2,$ and $c=50.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4(1)(50)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-200}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-196}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm 14\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\pm 7\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-1+7\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-1-7\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03456ac4-5798-56cc-8c14-0a645b2e232e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sum of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c5e0cbf-ada9-577c-acdb-c94f01edda6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c5e0cbf-ada9-577c-acdb-c94f01edda6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259303} :answer-field/id #uuid "8791452b-9f5e-5c66-a6eb-fc62a1552d1c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259302}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0273f815-5b6c-5f01-94fb-31a1e3f74724" :question/math-academy-id "q-288778" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-9x+2=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the sum of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=7,\\,b=-9,\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{a} & =-\\frac{(-9)}{7}=\\frac{9}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03456ac4-5798-56cc-8c14-0a645b2e232e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sum of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a949e2-f067-5d66-ab20-dc59a15d6625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22a949e2-f067-5d66-ab20-dc59a15d6625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259306} :answer-field/id #uuid "3efb9e91-8a8a-5d4a-b7c3-c9aea8cd6ba0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259305}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0b72192-2fad-5822-a291-598e7afc9350" :question/math-academy-id "q-288810" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+172x-15\\,300=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the sum of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=172,\\,c=-15\\,300.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{a} & =-\\frac{172}{1}=-172.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2044120-320b-5665-8071-29b32ad9ca08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2044120-320b-5665-8071-29b32ad9ca08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259309} :answer-field/id #uuid "263e2c7c-5f78-50d0-9f40-04493a057cdb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259308}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aaca8727-279c-5e4c-b712-d066e6241ce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-288813" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+11x-20=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=11,\\,c=-20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-20)}{4}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f16923e8-81d9-586a-aba2-f5c13f77c6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f16923e8-81d9-586a-aba2-f5c13f77c6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259312} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ce2755-627d-5f6b-adb2-bb7f7f424e14" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259311}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "939510b6-5bc7-5d08-b915-1376d3819fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-288814" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-5x-14=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=7,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-14)}{7}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367a65ee-1249-516a-9dfb-c4291f663555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\,041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "367a65ee-1249-516a-9dfb-c4291f663555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\,041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259315} :answer-field/id #uuid "5adcd2ec-d9e3-5e8d-8f85-09672d3a2271" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259314}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccac1a80-68f8-53f5-b849-c8a540e70082" :question/math-academy-id "q-288826" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+68x-1\\,041=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=68,\\,c=-1\\,041.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-1\\,041)}{1}=-1\\,041.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18c41022-916d-5d5c-89e6-ebfdc56f8fe3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Quadratic Equation With a Given Sum and Product of Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15dca4bb-ede0-5492-bd4c-7fc87173d3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+11x-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15dca4bb-ede0-5492-bd4c-7fc87173d3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+11x-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259318} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ffe486b-d471-5767-99f6-442998821574" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259317}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff802175-3c27-5ff7-bdc0-5910dfdc76cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-288849" :question/problem "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $1.$ The sum of the solutions equals $-11,$ and the product of the solutions equals $-28.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}\n$=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=1.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $-11.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-11 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ -11 & =-\\frac{b}{1} \\\\ 11 & =b \\\\ b & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $-28.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-28 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ -28 & =\\frac{c}{1} \\\\ c & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=11,\\,c=-28\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+11x-28=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18c41022-916d-5d5c-89e6-ebfdc56f8fe3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Quadratic Equation With a Given Sum and Product of Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e1bdaf3-4a29-5c1b-b2bd-a11780cc53a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-5x-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e1bdaf3-4a29-5c1b-b2bd-a11780cc53a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-5x-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259321} :answer-field/id #uuid "947f4549-fff6-56ca-b591-9ccc9b602b66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259320}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e950950-a915-54b2-8881-c84ae2ce175e" :question/math-academy-id "q-288850" :question/problem "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $1.$ The sum of the solutions equals $5,$ and the product of the solutions equals $-50.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}\n$=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=1.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $5.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ 5 & =-\\frac{b}{1} \\\\ -5 & =b \\\\ b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $-50.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ -50 & =\\frac{c}{1} \\\\ c & =-50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-50\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-5x-50=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7614a05-f221-5fda-b491-6893f44a1a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with Multiple Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1833f0b2-441a-57ec-ae57-6f9338dd96bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5b{c}^{2}}{6{a}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1833f0b2-441a-57ec-ae57-6f9338dd96bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5b{c}^{2}}{6{a}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259353} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8cab47-62d8-5b89-a9c0-22b792482cd4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259352}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c5939ab-138a-5087-be80-835026f210bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-29966" :question/problem "If $\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}}\\cdot X=\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c},$ then what is $X?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}}\\cdot X=\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c},$ we have\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c}÷\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c}÷\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c}\\cdot \\frac{25{c}^{3}}{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12\\cdot a{b}^{2}\\cdot b}{3\\cdot 5c}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot 5c\\cdot {c}^{2}}{12\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot a{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}\\cdot \\cancel{a{b}^{2}}\\cdot b}{3\\cdot \\cancel{5c}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{5c}\\cdot {c}^{2}}{\\cancel{12}\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot \\cancel{a{b}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{b}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5{c}^{2}}{2{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5b{c}^{2}}{6{a}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8cc1de8-5ac0-5607-a2eb-6eec375721e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c9d159-d38d-571e-8e23-96fca99598f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47c9d159-d38d-571e-8e23-96fca99598f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259533} :answer-field/id #uuid "f998fc59-ebd1-51ac-aad9-bf3636d66256" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259532}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81300025-2eb5-531e-a8ef-a34d25e65f22" :question/math-academy-id "q-319487" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=3+8\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fc3602d0d057902e1a510485ae8f02746afd80ea3bb7c2c8efc40ccfaf7b3dd4.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}(z)=arctan\\,(\\frac{8}{3})\\approx 1.21,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58558700-309c-52c4-af9e-3aa3f5fd3339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58558700-309c-52c4-af9e-3aa3f5fd3339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259536} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a0f0d6c-b5f9-5ac1-8827-380546e14c22" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259535}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a69aadf-c1cd-5b5a-91d9-c39dafc5090a" :question/math-academy-id "q-319495" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-7+5\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/86e7a97f9dcdec8a81d5362c79f15887da8ce13248c4758eafa49e960d8cf128.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{5}{7}) \\\\  & =0.620\\text{ }249\\text{ }…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi -0.620\\text{ }249\\text{ }…\\approx 2.52,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a560db4d-e9d3-5d89-b2cd-c886f32db9df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "618ba24b-c462-52a8-adae-c106bb4b969a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "618ba24b-c462-52a8-adae-c106bb4b969a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259539} :answer-field/id #uuid "e44e66c8-5155-5882-9deb-8d3e1978fda2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "730d6833-84c1-5541-960f-71582df5543b" :question/math-academy-id "q-319554" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-6\\sqrt{3}-6\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ea995ef688b12c8d58076fc832c9a5c735ff5933aff3ac6c347ae27850adbb2e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a560db4d-e9d3-5d89-b2cd-c886f32db9df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb8a91ea-8688-5b3e-b0ad-e48bc15c385a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb8a91ea-8688-5b3e-b0ad-e48bc15c385a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259542} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df36b26-9c4b-5f96-adef-b96e9ea08ef9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259541}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9947af32-4016-5a60-b152-40626695bde1" :question/math-academy-id "q-319558" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-3-3\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f6fb8f3f901796a1bc6320148c9c58295d45fadd4f1e5379588e89072e153f77.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "427f79d7-8cbe-5fad-9c19-bda31862cb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "427f79d7-8cbe-5fad-9c19-bda31862cb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259582} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be0c4e8-25bd-59ee-ae21-d0ce51fe84b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259581}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d0c7037-a792-5532-8a26-b8019a0ead40" :question/math-academy-id "q-321647" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=25{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-x-5$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $25,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 5$, and $\\pm 25.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{25}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{5}{5},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{5}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{25}, \\\\ \\pm 5,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{25},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052bb3bb-75e5-57ae-8dc4-4d7ca1d25494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "052bb3bb-75e5-57ae-8dc4-4d7ca1d25494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259595} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec7e4e7-60c7-5348-97e4-6b83eb41af42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef634c1b-9d3d-5d79-a276-a4667af5e630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef634c1b-9d3d-5d79-a276-a4667af5e630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259597} :answer-field/id #uuid "26471634-7f58-5bbf-b440-69354b9bced1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98b3f5f4-4a66-5a51-91f1-dc474368396a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98b3f5f4-4a66-5a51-91f1-dc474368396a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259599} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9f6c1c-9b24-557e-ac8d-62dfc574cbe3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259598}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4acf3abe-b1ce-5c44-b7f4-f5eee1633c9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-323611" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-10x-24$ has one of its roots in the interval $(2,4).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-24$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-24}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 12,\\,\\pm 24.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(2,4)$ is $3.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}+6x+8).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-3)({x}^{2}+6x+8) \\\\  & =(x-3)(x+2)(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263b2379-ca78-504d-b716-0871ff95c633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "263b2379-ca78-504d-b716-0871ff95c633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259602} :answer-field/id #uuid "233e0f41-f09e-5677-a93e-4488081bb7d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259601}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6dc510f3-7557-5168-b83b-b438937dc73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-323830" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=27{x}^{3}-54{x}^{2}+36x-8$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{2},1),$ the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ equals{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $27.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\pm \\frac{1}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{27},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{9}, \\\\  & \\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{9},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{3},\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ are $\\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{2}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $27$ | $-54$ | $36$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $18$ | $-24$ | $8$ |\n|  | $27$ | $-36$ | $12$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{2}{3})(27{x}^{2}-36x+12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{2}{3})(27{x}^{2}-36x+12) \\\\  & =3(x-\\frac{2}{3})(9{x}^{2}-12x+4) \\\\  & =3(x-\\frac{2}{3})(3x-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(3x-2)(3x-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(3x-2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "303d6888-a0f9-5d27-9bf3-15b38bc9ebd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "303d6888-a0f9-5d27-9bf3-15b38bc9ebd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259801} :answer-field/id #uuid "38611adc-fcfc-57f3-a7f8-c0d50374f43d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259800}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "399fc725-0978-537e-bb0c-8bab97a72cc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338377" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a086a1f616aee596a021ee4ef6bdc8723309cf8959719e9f55d6f20b845bd760.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=4.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3}={lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x))^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3))}^{2} \\\\  & ={((-2)^{2}+3)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(4+3)}^{2} \\\\  & ={7}^{2} \\\\  & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x))^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to -2}(5-f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & ={(5-4)}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3} & =49\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "852449ca-b4df-5142-9540-bb503d5b7b62" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Negative Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abdbd32d-1c31-5654-8c16-551742440db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{7/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abdbd32d-1c31-5654-8c16-551742440db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{7/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259840} :answer-field/id #uuid "99cdec2c-24ff-54e1-ae77-fb555b33d336" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259839}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e59a730f-20bd-5ba6-bc1c-41bc449c1743" :question/math-academy-id "q-339446" :question/problem "If $y=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-4/3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-4/3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-4/3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}){x}^{(-\\frac{4}{3}-1)} \\\\  & =2{x}^{-7/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{7}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80d72276-63b0-52c6-b7cf-0f4e2e0392aa" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions With a Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7183fd3d-190e-5d57-b5e7-71c60c45f256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-5a+2b}{6b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7183fd3d-190e-5d57-b5e7-71c60c45f256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-5a+2b}{6b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260156} :answer-field/id #uuid "267364fa-abf7-59ac-9da7-7ec3f03d2623" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260155}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b6a3c47-707d-5180-93af-4c625ca6bef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-3509" :question/problem "Give an expression equivalent to $\\frac{a-b}{6b}-\\frac{6a-3b}{6b}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $6b,$ so we subtract the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a-b}{6b}-\\frac{6a-3b}{6b} & = \\\\ \\frac{a-b-(6a-3b)}{6b} & = \\\\ \\frac{a-b-6a+3b}{6b} & = \\\\ \\frac{-5a+2b}{6b}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9bfd9e9-603e-50ae-8cd1-be79b88aa087" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Leading Coefficients as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c338ffc9-147b-5a7e-95d6-815775f54f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({r}^{2}+4)(r+2)(r-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c338ffc9-147b-5a7e-95d6-815775f54f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({r}^{2}+4)(r+2)(r-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260228} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4282184-b16c-5e57-9d04-02e18d0d9d1c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260227}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5639c612-ad13-56af-82f6-525e8cd4941e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37713" :question/problem "Factor the expression $2{r}^{4}-32.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms in our expression have a common factor of $2.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2{r}^{4}-32=2\\cdot {r}^{4}-2\\cdot 16=2({r}^{4}-16)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n2({r}^{4}-16)=2({({r}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n2({({r}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})=2({r}^{2}+4)({r}^{2}-4)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({r}^{2}+4)({r}^{2}-4) & =2({r}^{2}+4)({r}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({r}^{2}+4)(r+2)(r-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a55e754a-d7fb-5292-89ba-7ca6df0dc0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No maximum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a55e754a-d7fb-5292-89ba-7ca6df0dc0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No maximum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260347} :answer-field/id #uuid "b147741f-ed90-57c9-8ffb-d0d72e684bcd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260346}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7e52902-fed7-58d4-bb0e-42f5b74398ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-41246" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{x}{2}+1)-1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No maximum value\"." :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no maximum value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8058df30-04de-555c-adfa-61fbf2b0ff31" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in One Variable With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d9babea-ba75-5bf8-8a77-36ca409c93a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-p+6}{3(p+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d9babea-ba75-5bf8-8a77-36ca409c93a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-p+6}{3(p+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260353} :answer-field/id #uuid "5530c730-e55a-56ca-822d-c39163e22c21" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260352}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2619fe6-700e-5cff-9a2b-9c3c893461a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-41571" :question/problem "If $\\frac{p-2}{p+2}+X=\\frac{2p}{3p+6},$ then what is $X?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{p-2}{p+2}+X=\\frac{2p}{3p+6},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{2p}{3p+6}-\\frac{p-2}{p+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2p}{3(p+2)}-\\frac{p-2}{p+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $3(p+2)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{2p}{3(p+2)}-\\frac{p-2}{p+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2p}{3(p+2)}-\\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{p-2}{p+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2p}{3(p+2)}-\\frac{3(p-2)}{3(p+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2p-3(p-2)}{3(p+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2p-3p+6}{3(p+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-p+6}{3(p+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $X=\\frac{-p+6}{3(p+2)}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3060ff9-5832-5483-b4f6-621dd4a0844d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260835} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb093f3-6cd5-5f4f-9458-696e29118eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260836} {:answer/id #uuid "80d886b1-7b9b-5484-9d4b-45199d952142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260837} {:answer/id #uuid "d843c8f7-8868-5f25-bca3-74331a0671cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3060ff9-5832-5483-b4f6-621dd4a0844d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadrant II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260835} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc8f9d6b-fcbc-5fd8-9a0d-eb8398e0a69b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260834}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "838287b6-8054-530d-a9fc-68e77332ddb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-64685" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b5397fc107a7425c822a9d00a169fa7c3624a3e29e3a8266897d459f19ac1aee.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34724b94-1d6d-5ffc-b3ec-2e0b92812003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34724b94-1d6d-5ffc-b3ec-2e0b92812003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260853} :answer-field/id #uuid "4faf4288-afb2-5118-afd9-bf306bdd19d8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7497aaee-16af-5435-8a6f-690db33f8226" :question/math-academy-id "q-64921" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43750d40-b6a5-569a-9f14-594d1ce48907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43750d40-b6a5-569a-9f14-594d1ce48907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260856} :answer-field/id #uuid "424fca19-b484-52f5-9d03-9d3e8b433752" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b5a2976-278a-5503-aa48-0e6720682c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-64923" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93f296a3-9576-51c4-b33b-0f948582bc66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93f296a3-9576-51c4-b33b-0f948582bc66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260859} :answer-field/id #uuid "bacd49d1-4171-50e8-bfbf-31f75f685056" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260858}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8358751c-30a9-5482-b7a6-569bce8efae2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64929" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "852449ca-b4df-5142-9540-bb503d5b7b62" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Negative Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9885997e-1376-5536-a6a0-9722cc7a54d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9885997e-1376-5536-a6a0-9722cc7a54d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261204} :answer-field/id #uuid "6adcd29c-072f-59c1-a4c3-39a5ce0aee67" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261203}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a5cd98d-a47e-5b07-8017-c5a5ad2b0c53" :question/math-academy-id "q-70475" :question/problem "If $y=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}=-4{x}^{-1/2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{(-\\frac{1}{2}-1)} \\\\  & =2{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3469bdd-a201-5219-a734-ed3caa6b039a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions Without Constant Factors Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c243bbe-7b07-54af-a078-d2e6d4c52f51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c243bbe-7b07-54af-a078-d2e6d4c52f51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261207} :answer-field/id #uuid "22767839-57a2-5cad-9008-32377584c282" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261206}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c12fd860-5c97-5378-bf19-e5d7f29621d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70476" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+x-\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{(2-1)}+1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{1/2-1} \\\\  & =2x+1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3469bdd-a201-5219-a734-ed3caa6b039a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions Without Constant Factors Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80975c65-406a-559f-90b2-a40ed489ca78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80975c65-406a-559f-90b2-a40ed489ca78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261210} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e259fee-48cb-5733-b70f-ccb1c60eec5a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0973d8c1-d7f9-53f6-b5f7-d47fb1ce4f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-70512" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of two power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/5})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{(3/5-1)}-(-3){x}^{(-3-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{(-2/5)}+3{x}^{(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "add45606-39f6-5a61-9535-867d2950d183" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86c3d94b-9442-5e53-95b5-628d6cf14b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8{y}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c3d94b-9442-5e53-95b5-628d6cf14b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8{y}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261315} :answer-field/id #uuid "600e1514-57e4-554d-b897-c21018fba413" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261314}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b1354ad-3a99-5aa3-a7aa-bdaa08626a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71163" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{2{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{-5+1}}{-5+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{-4}}{-4})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {y}^{-4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{y}^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca7f6e86-a083-5dcd-beaa-8c3eae151bf1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "590cee47-7e84-546e-963d-8565c68d7fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No roots have the requested multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "590cee47-7e84-546e-963d-8565c68d7fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No roots have the requested multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261539} :answer-field/id #uuid "160afd1a-986c-54ab-8dfc-356007e7d3a6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b12024ad-e446-565a-b45f-00ebac6f638f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74161" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+32x+16$ have multiplicity $2?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No roots have the requested multiplicity\"." :question/worked-solution "We factor the cubic by grouping, and simplify until the polynomial is fully factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+32x+16 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x+1)+16(2x+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+16)(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the factor $({x}^{2}+16)$ cannot be factored any further, and it does not have any real roots.\n\nThe only real root of $p(x)$ comes from the factor $(2x+1).$ This factor has a root of $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the exponent of the factor is $1.$ Therefore, the root $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, there is no root with multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09beb145-1496-550f-b00a-df0dc48914bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09beb145-1496-550f-b00a-df0dc48914bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261542} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a72469-ad05-54ca-b423-8fd6c64ba2bd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261541}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac90a657-d015-5262-86eb-5d2eb167ec0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74234" :question/problem "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=-2$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+20x+16?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=-2$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+2)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $8$ | $20$ | $16$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-2$ | $-12$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x+2}={x}^{2}+6x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x+2)({x}^{2}+6x+8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x+2)(x+2)(x+4) \\\\  & =(x+4)(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+2)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-2$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f74ac24e-7373-571f-80bc-6aba6e0627c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f74ac24e-7373-571f-80bc-6aba6e0627c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261551} :answer-field/id #uuid "06ef4bfc-d47c-58cd-8f13-00bd0109c689" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261550}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90fbd631-4454-5ced-8abb-698546adad17" :question/math-academy-id "q-74664" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=-2+2\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b1e873f4efefc1a4cda57c7638b31137580757d302440230a8091b6850679073.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54588722-bb80-54ca-b100-03aca39e0c17" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7688cf-db4d-523a-adcb-cdd908aed875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b7688cf-db4d-523a-adcb-cdd908aed875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261563} :answer-field/id #uuid "dca7a1ee-9443-5081-953a-9dbbc85c3c46" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261562}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03b79ede-73f0-5a73-86a3-7340e27f6764" :question/math-academy-id "q-74726" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=5-5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/907a7699015953e2d57bb033d25f3bd3bb464f1b6c74fc5dd7cc0fc741942164.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{5}{5})=\\operatorname{arctan}(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80d72276-63b0-52c6-b7cf-0f4e2e0392aa" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions With a Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8840e4d-90c4-52f9-bc7d-0ef2f5a4a001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4k-3}{5k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8840e4d-90c4-52f9-bc7d-0ef2f5a4a001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4k-3}{5k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261753} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad245386-01a1-5ce7-8d93-bb3013687706" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261752}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a890f01-0653-5256-b369-62039cce320c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79542" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{k-4}{5k}+\\frac{1+3k}{5k}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $5k,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k-4}{5k}+\\frac{1+3k}{5k} & = \\\\ \\frac{k-4+1+3k}{5k} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-4+1)+(k+3k)}{5k} & = \\\\ \\frac{4k-3}{5k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb2231aa-8afa-57df-a43c-cb3add25b2cc" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639931b9-ef70-5a5d-afee-b29fbda88cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+2}{x(2y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639931b9-ef70-5a5d-afee-b29fbda88cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+2}{x(2y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261759} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f69c715-5f0d-5dd3-8117-e70846b49cd3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261758}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ad63e72-0278-582c-a463-b2f2be4b2bbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-79553" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{2}{x(2y+1)}+\\frac{3}{2y+1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $x(2y+1)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(2y+1)}+\\frac{3}{2y+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{x(2y+1)}+\\frac{x}{x}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2y+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{x(2y+1)}+\\frac{3x}{x(2y+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3x+2}{x(2y+1)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6f4cfa-db03-5ae6-9f43-79fd3167c473" :knowledge-point/title "The Vertical Asymptote of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3dd86d6-af05-5fd9-93fd-d14288f5afc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3dd86d6-af05-5fd9-93fd-d14288f5afc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261829} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae790934-5671-5c0e-909f-7b08340e6f51" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261828}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44f8aeb9-7b9b-5545-a9d7-81c594cad411" :question/math-academy-id "q-81989" :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{ln}(x-2).$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6f4cfa-db03-5ae6-9f43-79fd3167c473" :knowledge-point/title "The Vertical Asymptote of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4be54b7-c1c7-57e7-b65b-d57e279f5879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4be54b7-c1c7-57e7-b65b-d57e279f5879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261832} :answer-field/id #uuid "f573ca18-22e7-51aa-a940-c791b35f611d" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261831}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "348bca57-b4c9-5af6-8669-2cc5f0e2a061" :question/math-academy-id "q-81990" :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(2x)-8.$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y=\\operatorname{log}x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x)$, we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30905c28-cb3e-546b-9dc8-5fce5d94c32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30905c28-cb3e-546b-9dc8-5fce5d94c32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261884} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1aef993-e83d-5c8c-b93e-e98f5353e4cc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261883}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a045cc6b-4cfa-5c4d-a695-16b0b2a1fa9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83815" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, What **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+x-3?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec0a9244-0323-5c50-9ce3-be04162b7b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec0a9244-0323-5c50-9ce3-be04162b7b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261887} :answer-field/id #uuid "82ea7c62-6cec-5fea-9f67-89017e02bd87" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261886}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c03b7d9a-1305-5730-b73a-a062c9cbaa05" :question/math-academy-id "q-83863" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 4,\\pm 8\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ are $-4$ and $-2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-4$ | $12$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+4)({x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & =(x+4)(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-4,1,2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-4+1+2=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6699397d-0014-5749-b439-4a8486990986" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "434ec09f-ccb5-5c0a-8667-6647f9cb6dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "434ec09f-ccb5-5c0a-8667-6647f9cb6dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261925} :answer-field/id #uuid "000dbb85-f45d-5ae1-a493-4f7966ffcde0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261924}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc09cb6b-cb50-55e8-8667-d714b407a48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-85514" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${30}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}]}
